WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:08.302
This is Anthropic, Read Aloud. Today: A I for scientific discovery: How researchers use Claude.

2
00:00:09.602 --> 00:00:13.204
A I for scientific discovery: How researchers use Claude.

3
00:00:13.654 --> 00:00:33.238
In this report, we use privacy-preserving infrastructure to study the scientific use of Claude, Claude Code, and Claude Science. We document how usage varies across scientific fields and research tasks, identify what explains these usage patterns, and outline scenarios for how A I could accelerate research.

4
00:00:33.688 --> 00:00:48.607
From Anthropic Research, published November 5, 2026. By Johannes Hermle, Ben Jones, Lukas Althoff, Ryan Heller, Thomas Lyttelton, Carolyn Stein, Nathan Wilmers, Maxim Massenkoff, and Peter McCrory.

5
00:00:49.607 --> 00:00:50.255
Summary.

6
00:00:50.705 --> 00:01:10.369
What we measure. A I could speed up scientific discovery, but by how much depends on what A I can do and how scientists use it. Using privacy-preserving methods, we study 207000 Claude and Claude Code research sessions to understand how Claude is used across scientific fields and research tasks.

7
00:01:10.489 --> 00:01:21.360
We are conducting this research to help the public better understand the state of A I for science today and assess the impact that A I may have on scientific discovery in the future.

8
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:40.113
Where scientists use Claude. Relative to the number of researchers in each field, Claude is used more in the formal sciences, such as computer science, mathematics, and statistics, and less in health and lab-based physical sciences. Scientists primarily use Claude to analyze data and to write.

9
00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:58.434
Data analysis alone accounts for about one-third of sessions on Claude and nearly half of sessions on Claude Code. Scientists use it less for idea generation and data gathering, even though, in an accompanying survey, they rank those tasks as the most important for scientific discovery.

10
00:01:58.554 --> 00:02:16.657
That said, specialized A I tools designed for scientific work could narrow the gap between the research tasks scientists use A I for and the ones they consider most important. On Claude Science, for instance, idea generation makes up about twice the share of use that it does on Claude.

11
00:02:16.897 --> 00:02:34.638
How Claude use tracks A I capability. We create a measure of how well A I can, in theory, perform each research task. The tasks A I is best suited to (the top 20 percent on this measure) get two to four times as much Claude use as the time researchers spend on them would predict.

12
00:02:34.758 --> 00:02:50.740
The tasks A I is least suited to (the bottom 20 percent), which include most physical work, get very little. We observe the same pattern across fields: fields where A I can perform many tasks well, such as computer science and statistics, use Claude more.

13
00:02:50.980 --> 00:03:05.913
What this could mean for the speed of scientific discovery. Using a simple model, we explore scenarios for how much A I could speed up research. If existing A I capabilities are widely adopted, they could double research output in some fields.

14
00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:24.112
If A I could carry out all non-physical research work, output in fields such as computer science and statistics could rise as much as 15-fold, while output in bench sciences such as chemistry and biology, which are held back by physical bottlenecks, would rise three- or fourfold.

15
00:03:24.232 --> 00:03:34.642
In our survey of researchers who actively use A I, about 40 percent expect A I models to carry out full research projects on their own by 2030.

16
00:03:35.442 --> 00:03:36.286
Introduction.

17
00:03:36.736 --> 00:03:50.234
Science has been described as an “endless frontier” (Bush 1945). But how fast we explore that frontier has historically been limited by a scarce resource: the time and talent of human researchers.

18
00:03:50.354 --> 00:04:08.425
Artificial intelligence could ease that limit by amplifying what researchers can do, transforming both the process of discovery and the pace of exploration (Wang and others 2023; Amodei 2024). Few applications of A I could matter more for human welfare.

19
00:04:08.545 --> 00:04:17.690
Given the highly cognitive nature of research compared with most sectors of the economy, A I’s effects may be particularly large and early in this domain.

20
00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:48.289
Recent examples show A I playing a growing role in scientific discovery. In structural biology, AlphaFold predicted the three-dimensional shapes of over 200 million proteins (Jumper and others 2021; Varadi and others 2024), and its creators shared the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. In materials science, GNoME predicted 380000 stable new crystals (Merchant and others 2023).

21
00:04:48.409 --> 00:05:14.195
In mathematics, researchers have used A I systems to help resolve decades-old Erdős problems, produce a potential proof for a Millennium Prize problem, and make progress on other major problems, from open conjectures to verifying landmark proofs (Anthropic 2026a, 2026b; OpenAI 2026a, 2026b; Tsoukalas and others 2026).

22
00:05:14.645 --> 00:05:33.288
Beyond these developments, most scientific progress involves many specialized, interlinked research tasks, which can vary substantially across fields. How much A I accelerates scientific discovery depends not only on A I’s capabilities, but also on how it is adopted across these tasks.

23
00:05:33.408 --> 00:05:51.877
In this report, we use privacy-preserving infrastructure to study the scientific use of Claude, Claude Code, and Claude Science. We document how usage varies across fields and tasks, identify what explains these usage patterns, and outline scenarios for how A I could accelerate research.

24
00:05:52.327 --> 00:06:16.292
Claude use in science varies by field. Relative to their number of researchers, formal sciences such as computer science, mathematics, and statistics use Claude more heavily, while health and lab-based physical sciences use it less. Across research tasks, scientists use Claude more for analyzing data and writing, and less for gathering data or generating ideas and hypotheses.

25
00:06:16.412 --> 00:06:21.873
This is consistent with self-reported A I use from an accompanying survey of scientists.

26
00:06:22.323 --> 00:06:44.382
To understand these differences, we develop a measure of theoretical A I capability. Claude use is higher in tasks and fields that score higher on this measure. These also tend to be more cognitive in nature and better suited to reinforcement learning, in which A I learns from feedback on repeated attempts. In short, scientists appear to use A I where it works best.

27
00:06:44.832 --> 00:07:02.528
Based on these findings, we outline scenarios for how A I could accelerate scientific progress. With current capabilities, research output in some fields could as much as double, primarily from task-level augmentation. Researchers in our survey expect much greater capabilities to come.

28
00:07:02.648 --> 00:07:15.086
In a hypothetical scenario where A I fully automates all non-physical work, highly cognitive fields such as computer science or statistics could see up to a 15-fold increase in output.

29
00:07:15.206 --> 00:07:31.313
Because the fields with the largest potential for acceleration include those that produce A I itself, these gains could create an additional feedback loop, in which faster A I research produces more capable A I, which in turn could further accelerate research.

30
00:07:31.763 --> 00:07:45.885
While faster scientific discovery could bring substantial benefits to humanity, A I-driven research also carries risks and uncertainties. Less human oversight could lead to the pursuit of dangerous or unethical research.

31
00:07:46.005 --> 00:07:55.871
A I could also reshape researchers’ careers, making research work more or less rewarding, and alter the sense of purpose associated with the human pursuit of knowledge.

32
00:07:56.671 --> 00:07:58.383
How we conducted this research.

33
00:07:58.833 --> 00:08:13.397
Our goal with this research is to measure the different ways scientists use Claude while protecting the details of their work. To do this, we use privacy-preserving infrastructure to analyze the scientific use of Claude on consumer plans.

34
00:08:13.517 --> 00:08:31.383
A language model, used here as a “classifier,” reads each session transcript and labels it with a fixed set of categories, such as the field and the research task it involves, according to a predefined taxonomy (SciNet taxonomy by Alamos and Althoff 2026).

35
00:08:31.503 --> 00:08:42.061
The resulting labels, such as “chemistry” or “data cleaning,” are the only information from these conversations retained and analyzed by the research team for this report.

36
00:08:42.181 --> 00:09:02.875
No classifier employed in this study extracts and stores open-ended information or details on research content, such as research datasets, hypotheses, experimental methods, early findings, or unpublished papers. Results are reported only in aggregate and only for cells that meet a minimum number of distinct accounts.

37
00:09:02.995 --> 00:09:06.954
All classifiers introduced in this study are listed in the Appendix.

38
00:09:07.404 --> 00:09:30.080
Our measurement involves two steps. A first classifier identifies whether a session transcript involves scientific work. A second classifier then assigns each of these transcripts to a field, subfield, and task in the SciNet taxonomy. SciNet contains 7262 tasks, spanning 34 fields and 318 subfields.

39
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:52.808
Some tasks in the taxonomy, such as drafting manuscripts, are universal to all fields. Others are specific to individual fields or subfields. For instance, in the taxonomy, the task of predicting optical absorption and emission spectra is specific to materials science. Together, the tasks cover the full process of producing a new research finding.

40
00:09:53.258 --> 00:10:18.925
Our main sample consists of 207049 sessions on consumer plans from August 2 to September 30, 2026, which were assigned a scientific field and a research task: 128914 from Chat and Cowork, two general-purpose interfaces for conversation and knowledge work, hereafter referred to as “Claude,” and 78135 from Claude Code,

41
00:10:19.045 --> 00:10:24.269
an agentic coding tool that works directly with a user’s files, writing and running code.

42
00:10:24.389 --> 00:10:31.856
We also study a supplemental sample of Claude Science sessions. For details on sample construction, see the Appendix.

43
00:10:32.306 --> 00:10:59.357
Combining usage data and a structured taxonomy of research tasks allows us to study which fields adopt Claude and for which parts of the research process. This research contributes to a growing body of work attempting to measure the adoption of A I technology for scientific discovery (Kobak and others 2025; Liang and others 2025; Hao and others 2026; Codreanu and others 2026).

44
00:11:00.157 --> 00:11:02.820
How Claude use varies across scientific fields.

45
00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:17.696
We begin by describing how Claude use varies across scientific fields. Figure 1 compares a field’s share of sessions against the share of global researchers in that field, giving us a sense of relative use per scientific field.

46
00:11:17.816 --> 00:11:43.262
1 On Claude, the three largest fields by session share are computer science (11.9 percent), engineering (8.4 percent), and medicine (7.7 percent), which are also among the largest fields by global researcher counts. 2 Overall, fields with a larger share of researchers also tend to account for a larger share of Claude sessions (log-log correlation of 0.79).

47
00:11:43.712 --> 00:12:07.646
However, Claude use per field cannot be explained by the number of researchers alone. In Figure 1, fields above the 45-degree line show higher usage relative to their size, while fields below the line show lower usage. Relative usage is especially high for the formal sciences, including computer science, mathematics, and statistics. Usage is also high for the social sciences.

48
00:12:07.766 --> 00:12:22.891
By contrast, physical sciences such as chemistry, where work often happens in a lab, tend to show lower relative usage. So does medicine, the third-largest field by session volume, where researchers spend much of their time with patients.

49
00:12:23.341 --> 00:12:44.052
On Claude Code, usage is less strongly related to field size (log-log correlation with researcher counts of 0.62) and concentrates in more quantitative fields. Despite making up only 4.3 percent of the world’s researchers, computer science alone accounts for 32.7 percent of Claude Code sessions.

50
00:12:44.172 --> 00:13:00.097
3 Other formal sciences, such as mathematics and statistics, also show high relative usage. Relative usage is low in the arts and humanities and in the social sciences, with the exception of economics, a comparatively quantitative field.

51
00:13:00.547 --> 00:13:20.169
Figure 1: Claude use across scientific fields. Each dot represents a field. The horizontal axis shows the field’s share of researchers, and the vertical axis shows the field’s share of research sessions, both on log scales. The top panel shows sessions from Claude; the bottom panel shows sessions from Claude Code.

52
00:13:20.289 --> 00:13:40.093
Image description: Two scatter plots, one for Claude and one for Claude Code, plotting each scientific field’s share of research sessions against its share of researchers on log scales, with a dashed parity line. The log-log correlation is +0.79 on Claude and +0.62 on Claude Code.

53
00:13:40.213 --> 00:13:58.590
On Claude, computer science, economics, and statistics sit well above the line, while medicine and clinical sciences, nutrition and dietetics, and veterinary medicine sit below it. On Claude Code, computer science is far above the line, and most arts and humanities fields fall below it.

54
00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:10.172
Bar charts beside each plot list the top 10 fields by session volume, led by computer science (11.9 percent on Claude, 32.7 percent on Claude Code).

55
00:14:10.622 --> 00:14:34.545
We observe similar patterns when comparing usage across 318 subfields within those 34 fields (Figure 2). 4 This more granular subfield view reveals that artificial intelligence and machine learning stands out, with session shares of 5.0 percent for Claude and 15.2 percent for Claude Code, despite making up only 1.2 percent of the world’s researchers.

56
00:14:34.665 --> 00:14:58.402
More generally, regardless of the broad scientific domain, computational subfields are among the strongest adopters. These subfields include computational biology, computational linguistics, and computational neuroscience. Robotics also shows high relative usage, likely because it builds heavily on computational methods despite its focus on physical systems.

57
00:14:58.852 --> 00:15:19.349
Figure 2: Claude use across scientific subfields. Each dot represents a subfield. The horizontal axis shows the subfield’s share of researchers, and the vertical axis shows the subfield’s share of research sessions, both on log scales. The top panel shows sessions from Claude; the bottom panel shows sessions from Claude Code.

58
00:15:19.469 --> 00:15:42.764
Subfields with too few accounts are not shown for privacy reasons. Image description: Two scatter plots, one for Claude and one for Claude Code, plotting each scientific subfield’s share of research sessions against its share of researchers on log scales, with a dashed parity line. The log-log correlation is +0.61 on Claude and +0.43 on Claude Code.

59
00:15:42.884 --> 00:16:01.712
Labeled subfields above the line include artificial intelligence and machine learning, bioinformatics and computational biology, econometrics, biostatistics, and computational linguistics on Claude, and computational materials science and computational neuroscience on Claude Code.

60
00:16:01.832 --> 00:16:13.631
Bar charts beside each plot list the top 10 subfields by session volume, led by A I and machine learning (5.0 percent on Claude, 15.2 percent on Claude Code).

61
00:16:14.431 --> 00:16:17.018
How Claude use varies across research tasks.

62
00:16:17.468 --> 00:16:38.816
Beyond understanding which fields use Claude, we also want to understand what scientists use it for. Research involves many different tasks, from generating hypotheses and analyzing data to writing up findings in a research paper. To better discern how Claude supports scientific discovery, we study how usage varies across these tasks.

63
00:16:38.936 --> 00:17:00.774
To do so, we assign each session to one of the 7262 tasks in SciNet. These tasks can be universal to all fields, such as drafting manuscripts, or specific to fields or subfields, such as performing differential gene expression analysis to compare tumor subtypes, which is associated with cancer biology.

64
00:17:00.894 --> 00:17:13.616
The taxonomy distinguishes between research tasks, which researchers perform to produce new scientific findings, and non-research tasks, such as teaching, peer review, and service to the department or field.

65
00:17:13.736 --> 00:17:37.854
5 We limit our analysis to research tasks spanning eight categories: (1) data analysis, (2) writing and communication, (3) data gathering, (4) reading and knowledge acquisition, (5) theoretical analysis, (6) design and development (of software, materials, and creative works), (7) ideation and hypothesis generation, and (8) other, such as administering research.

66
00:17:38.304 --> 00:17:54.004
For each task category, we calculate its share of Claude and Claude Code sessions separately. To assess whether usage in a category is high or low, we consider the share of their time that researchers typically spend on that category, as estimated by SciNet.

67
00:17:54.124 --> 00:18:09.548
These time estimates are based on an L L M classifier, validated using both wet-lab research protocols that record researcher time spent per task and the frequency of tasks across thousands of academic papers (see the Appendix ).

68
00:18:09.668 --> 00:18:19.010
This provides a benchmark to assess whether the share of Claude use across different task categories follows or deviates from how researchers spend their time.

69
00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:40.329
Usage by task category. Data analysis tasks have the highest usage in absolute terms and are overrepresented relative to researcher time. Data analysis accounts for 33.7 percent of sessions on Claude and 48.1 percent on Claude Code, compared with 27.6 percent of researcher time.

70
00:18:40.449 --> 00:19:04.453
Writing and communication tasks also show high usage, especially on Claude (32.0 percent, compared with 16.0 percent of researcher time). 6 Data gathering tasks have low relative usage, accounting for 28.6 percent of researcher time but only 11.5 percent of sessions on Claude and 11.9 percent on Claude Code.

71
00:19:04.573 --> 00:19:13.929
The underrepresentation of data gathering is particularly strong for tasks with physical barriers, such as the collection of clinical or biochemical data.

72
00:19:14.049 --> 00:19:28.993
Data gathering tasks that can rely on more digital processes, such as retrieving archival documents or text, are less underrepresented, while purely digital data collection tasks, such as collecting online data, can be overrepresented.

73
00:19:29.113 --> 00:19:43.107
Ideation and hypothesis generation tasks also have low usage, accounting for 4.2 percent of sessions on Claude and 2.1 percent on Claude Code, compared with 7.1 percent of researcher time.

74
00:19:43.557 --> 00:20:06.555
Figure 3: Claude use by scientific task category. Each row represents one of the eight task categories. Orange bars represent the share of Claude research sessions dedicated to each category; blue bars represent the same for Claude Code research sessions. The black lines represent the estimated share of research time calculated from SciNet (see the Appendix ).

75
00:20:06.675 --> 00:20:32.457
Image description: Horizontal bar chart of the share of research sessions in each of eight task categories on Claude and Claude Code, with a line marking each category’s share of research time. Data analysis: 33.7 percent on Claude and 48.1 percent on Claude Code. Writing and communication: 32.0 percent and 17.6 percent. Data gathering: 11.5 percent and 11.9 percent.

76
00:20:32.577 --> 00:20:59.968
Reading and knowledge acquisition: 7.6 percent and 3.7 percent. Theoretical analysis: 5.0 percent and 4.7 percent. Design and development: 4.7 percent and 7.7 percent. Ideation and hypothesis generation: 4.2 percent and 2.1 percent. Other: 1.3 percent and 4.2 percent. Data gathering and ideation fall well below their share of research time.

77
00:21:00.418 --> 00:21:23.204
Comparing observed usage to survey evidence. Along with our analysis of Claude and Claude Code sessions, we conducted a survey of 528 scientists, whose self-reported A I usage lines up with the patterns we observed in those sessions. The sample consists of researchers primarily in the life and health sciences who are active adopters of A I.

78
00:21:23.324 --> 00:21:47.386
7 Consistent with the Claude usage patterns shown in Figure 3, survey participants’ self-reported A I usage is highest for data analysis and writing and communication, and lower for ideation and hypothesis generation and data gathering. Participants also report more shared or delegated use of A I for data analysis and writing and communication (Figure 4).

79
00:21:47.836 --> 00:22:09.553
The survey also asked which categories matter most for producing important research results. Respondents rank ideation and hypothesis generation first and data gathering second. Notably, these categories are underrepresented in self-reported usage as well as in Claude sessions. The reasons for this underrepresentation likely differ.

80
00:22:09.673 --> 00:22:29.462
Much of data gathering is physical, while ideation is not. For ideation, scientists may prefer generating ideas themselves, or they may perceive A I’s capabilities as limited. Our data may also undercount ideation, since it tends to be woven into many other research tasks and is difficult to classify.

81
00:22:29.582 --> 00:22:38.893
But A I may also be genuinely weaker at high-level ideation, such as choosing which research question to pursue and designing a study to answer it.

82
00:22:39.343 --> 00:23:02.234
Figure 4: Researchers’ reported use of A I. The left panel shows the percentage of survey respondents at each level of A I use, from none to autonomous, for four research task categories. The right panel shows how the respondents rate the importance of each category for producing an important research result (allocating 100 points across the four categories).

83
00:23:02.354 --> 00:23:24.259
Image description: Two-panel chart of survey results for four research task categories. Left: stacked bars of the percentage of respondents at each level of A I use (none, assistive, shared, delegated, autonomous). Ideation and hypothesis generation: 17 percent none, 53 percent assistive, 24 percent shared.

84
00:23:24.379 --> 00:23:50.202
Data gathering: 20 percent none, 31 percent assistive, 30 percent shared, 14 percent delegated. Data analysis: 20 percent assistive, 44 percent shared, 28 percent delegated. Writing and communication: 40 percent assistive, 41 percent shared, 13 percent delegated. Right: bar chart of mean importance points out of 100. Ideation and hypothesis generation: 35.6.

85
00:23:50.322 --> 00:23:58.501
Data gathering: 23.6. Data analysis: 23.3. Writing and communication: 17.5.

86
00:23:58.951 --> 00:24:10.773
Biological and biomedical use of Claude Science. While Claude and Claude Code are general-purpose interfaces, specialized A I tools for the sciences are increasingly available.

87
00:24:10.893 --> 00:24:25.067
To study whether such tools support more specialized and demanding research, we use our privacy-preserving analysis infrastructure to compare biology and biomedical work on Claude Science with that on Claude and Claude Code.

88
00:24:25.517 --> 00:24:45.547
Claude Science connects Claude to many research tools and databases that are otherwise fragmented and acts to integrate and accelerate scientific workflows. Scientists must often use different platforms, each with unique data structures or query languages, to combine distinct tools and databases in their research.

89
00:24:45.667 --> 00:25:04.665
For example, biologists studying whether existing drugs can act on a specific protein must typically consult separate databases regarding the protein’s function, its structure, compounds already tested against it, and related drug trials or papers. Claude Science combines these into one workflow.

90
00:25:05.115 --> 00:25:22.872
Use of Claude Science is concentrated in the life sciences. The top two fields are biomedical sciences and biology, which together comprise over 50 percent of sessions. Within these fields, we assess which tasks are most overrepresented relative to tasks performed by Claude.

91
00:25:23.322 --> 00:25:40.869
We find that researchers use Claude Science for more specialized tasks than Claude (Figure 5). For example, the task “Propose mechanistic models for protein to protein or protein to ligand interactions” is 5.6 x more likely to appear on Claude Science than on Claude.

92
00:25:40.989 --> 00:26:03.037
This task involves explaining, at the level of individual molecular contacts, how a protein binds to another protein or to a small molecule such as a drug. Claude Science is well positioned for this task, as it can gather protein structures and binding data from multiple connected databases and combine them into a testable model of the interaction.

93
00:26:03.157 --> 00:26:11.832
By contrast, Claude Science is used relatively less for general tasks such as drafting manuscripts or coordinating with collaborators.

94
00:26:11.952 --> 00:26:29.367
Moreover, we find that ideation and hypothesis generation accounts for 8 percent of Claude Science sessions, roughly on par with the share of researcher time these tasks take, suggesting that specialized A I tools for science can facilitate more complex and creative work.

95
00:26:29.817 --> 00:26:39.613
Figure 5: Claude Science use across fields and tasks. The top panel shows the top 10 fields by their share of all Claude Science research sessions.

96
00:26:39.733 --> 00:26:59.768
The bottom panel shows the most and least typical tasks on Claude Science relative to Claude within biology and biomedical sciences specifically, calculated as follows: (task’s share of Claude Science research sessions in these fields) / (its share of Claude research sessions in these fields). Image description: Two-panel chart.

97
00:26:59.888 --> 00:27:19.301
Top: bar chart of the top 10 fields by share of Claude Science research sessions, led by biomedical sciences (31.1 percent), biology (24.1 percent), and medicine and clinical sciences (8.7 percent), with every other field below 5 percent.

98
00:27:19.421 --> 00:27:43.864
Bottom: chart of the most and least typical tasks on Claude Science relative to Claude within biology and biomedical sciences, on a log scale. Most typical: hypotheses on protein structure and function (6.0 x), models of protein to protein and protein to ligand interaction (5.6 x), and hypotheses on drug to target interactions (5.6 x).

99
00:27:43.984 --> 00:27:58.512
Least typical: coordinating with collaborators (0.15 x), presenting findings at conferences (0.2 x), and analyzing species abundance and diversity data (0.23 x).

100
00:27:58.962 --> 00:28:19.651
Focusing on biomedical sciences and biology, and using measures from our economic primitives framework introduced in prior work (Appel and others 2026), we find that tasks on Claude Science are longer, more sophisticated, and performed more autonomously than those on Claude and Claude Code (Figure 6).

101
00:28:19.771 --> 00:28:37.960
This is consistent with specialized A I tools being used for more demanding tasks. 8 Sessions on Claude Science contain work that would take nearly twice as many human hours to complete without A I as work on Claude and Claude Code. The content of these sessions is also more advanced.

102
00:28:38.080 --> 00:29:02.160
Over 80 percent of Claude Science use is estimated to require doctoral-level training (20 years of education), compared with about 50 to 70 percent on other interfaces. Over 70 percent of Claude Science sessions are performed with a high degree of A I autonomy, far higher than on Claude (23.7 percent) and somewhat higher than on Claude Code (64.1 percent).

103
00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:19.271
Figure 6: Use characteristics in biology and biomedical sciences. Comparing human task time, doctoral-level content, and level of autonomy for tasks completed on Claude, Claude Code, and Claude Science in biology and biomedical sciences.

104
00:29:19.391 --> 00:29:43.178
The hatched orange and blue bars reweight the subfields and tasks performed by Claude and Claude Code to match those performed by Claude Science. Image description: Three bar charts comparing Claude, Claude Code, and Claude Science in biology and biomedical sciences. Time for a human alone: 4.9 hours on Claude, 5.9 on Claude Code, and 10.3 on Claude Science.

105
00:29:43.298 --> 00:30:19.602
Doctoral-level content: 48.0 percent, 67.7 percent, and 82.7 percent of research sessions. High A I autonomy: 23.7 percent, 64.1 percent, and 73.3 percent of research sessions. Hatched bars, reweighted to the subfields and tasks of Claude Science, show similar values for Claude (5.1 hours, 55.1 percent, 25.2 percent) and Claude Code (5.5 hours, 71.8 percent, 61.4 percent).

106
00:30:20.052 --> 00:30:38.479
Outputs of Claude use. Finally, the artifacts produced with Claude show clear differences across fields (Figure 7). 9 Written artifacts such as documents, reports, or academic papers are more common in the social sciences and humanities, relative to the natural and formal sciences.

107
00:30:38.599 --> 00:31:01.446
Conversely, technical artifacts such as code, software outputs, and data are more frequent in the natural and formal sciences. The health sciences overall are more similar to the social sciences in terms of artifacts produced. Technical artifacts are about twice as common on Claude Code as on Claude, but the broad pattern across fields is similar (see the Appendix ).

108
00:31:01.896 --> 00:31:16.464
Figure 7: Artifacts produced by Claude. The share of sessions on Claude that produce written artifacts and technical artifacts by field. Fields are ordered by the difference between the share of written artifacts and technical artifacts.

109
00:31:16.584 --> 00:31:38.302
Image description: Dot plot of the percentage of each field’s research sessions on Claude that produce written artifacts and technical artifacts. Across all research, 52 percent of sessions produce written artifacts and 21 percent produce technical artifacts. Biology is the only field where technical artifacts are more common than written ones.

110
00:31:38.422 --> 00:31:45.819
The gap is narrowest in physics and astronomy and in statistics, and widest in law, education, and anthropology.

111
00:31:46.619 --> 00:31:48.517
How use relates to capabilities.

112
00:31:48.967 --> 00:32:10.556
Does A I usage track A I capabilities? Field size explains some of the variation in Claude use, but not all of it. For example, researchers in computer science use Claude far more than the field’s size predicts, while those in nutrition use Claude far less. Similarly, where researchers spend their own time does not fully predict where they use Claude the most.

113
00:32:10.676 --> 00:32:30.014
Some broad task categories, such as data analysis and writing, have a much larger share of usage than of research time, while others, such as data gathering and ideation and hypothesis generation, have a much smaller share. Here, we test whether these differences in usage reflect what A I can actually do.

114
00:32:30.464 --> 00:32:54.136
To measure task-specific A I capabilities, we first construct capability scores using an L L M classifier. For each task, SciNet provides a list of individual steps that a scientist needs to perform to complete the task. For instance, in chemistry, one task involves purifying reaction mixtures, and one of that task’s steps involves packing a chromatography column.

115
00:32:54.256 --> 00:33:14.926
The classifier is shown pairs of steps across the taxonomy and asked which one A I can perform better. We aggregate these comparisons using a Bradley to Terry model, the same model behind the Elo ratings that rank chess players based on head-to-head results. A step’s score rises when it wins comparisons and falls when it loses them.

116
00:33:15.046 --> 00:33:28.333
We then average each task’s steps to obtain a task-level score (see the Appendix for details). We label this measure theoretical A I capability. We refer to a task’s specific score as its A I capability score.

117
00:33:28.783 --> 00:33:48.635
To compare usage across tasks, we ask whether Claude use is higher or lower for a task, relative to the time researchers spend on that task. For example, a task that takes 5 percent of researchers’ time but accounts for 10 percent of Claude sessions would represent twice as much use as expected, a ratio of 2.

118
00:33:48.755 --> 00:34:03.006
A ratio of 1 indicates that Claude use is in line with researcher time spent on the task. A ratio below 1 indicates that Claude is used less than expected based on the amount of researcher time the task takes.

119
00:34:03.456 --> 00:34:25.689
Figure 8 shows that this use ratio rises sharply with a task’s A I capability score. For tasks in the lowest 20 percent of capability scores, like purifying reaction mixtures and conducting participant observation, Claude is barely used. At median capability scores, the ratio is roughly 1, so usage is in line with researcher time.

120
00:34:25.809 --> 00:34:47.625
For tasks in the top 20 percent of capability scores, like visualizing data and applying statistical tests, usage is about twice as high on Claude and about four times as high on Claude Code, compared with the typical time researchers spend on those tasks. These patterns suggest that scientists’ use of Claude closely tracks A I capability scores.

121
00:34:47.745 --> 00:34:54.785
The steeper gradient for Claude Code reflects its focus on computational work, which has higher capability scores.

122
00:34:55.235 --> 00:35:18.456
Figure 8: Claude use and theoretical A I capability. The vertical axis represents the use ratio for the tasks in each capability quintile, calculated as the share of research sessions devoted to those tasks divided by their share of research time estimated from SciNet. The horizontal axis represents the task’s A I capability score (percentile).

123
00:35:18.576 --> 00:35:39.650
The orange dots represent the average use ratio for tasks in each A I capability quintile for Claude; the blue dots represent the same for Claude Code. Image description: Line chart of the use ratio (share of use divided by share of research time) by the theoretical A I capability percentile of the task, for Claude and Claude Code.

124
00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:54.138
Both lines rise from about 0.2 in the lowest capability quintile to about 1 in the middle quintile. In the highest quintile, the ratio reaches about 2.1 on Claude and about 3.9 on Claude Code.

125
00:35:54.588 --> 00:35:58.928
Theoretical A I capabilities can differ across tasks for many reasons.

126
00:35:59.048 --> 00:36:22.450
To understand these differences, we relate each task’s A I capability score to three groups of L L M-derived task characteristics: its suitability for reinforcement learning (R L); whether the work is predominantly cognitive, interpersonal, or physical; and whether regulatory restrictions limit A I use (Figure 9; see the Appendix for definitions and data sources).

127
00:36:22.900 --> 00:36:33.749
Consider R L suitability. In R L, an A I model tries a task repeatedly and is rewarded when it succeeds, allowing the model to learn how to perform that task.

128
00:36:33.869 --> 00:36:57.699
Tasks are particularly suitable for R L when success is easy to verify, when many examples can be simulated in a computing environment, and when feedback can be delivered quickly (Lambert and others 2024; Guo and others 2025). We use task-specific measures from SciNet for these three R L-related components and summarize them in a single index.

129
00:36:57.819 --> 00:37:13.431
A task’s capability score is highly correlated with its R L index score, which was classified separately (correlation of 0.78). This suggests that A I is most capable at the research tasks where R L is easiest to apply.

130
00:37:13.881 --> 00:37:34.591
Other important task characteristics concern the nature of the work. Some research tasks are fundamentally cognitive, while others require physical actions or interpersonal contact. Physical tasks in general are beyond the reach of purely cognitive machines. There can also be regulatory restrictions that limit A I use.

131
00:37:34.711 --> 00:38:01.823
A I capability scores are higher for tasks characterized by cognitive work (correlation of 0.69). By contrast, research tasks that involve more physical work have lower A I capability scores (-0.57). Scores also tend to be lower for research activities that include interpersonal work (-0.31) or that involve regulatory restrictions on A I use (-0.38).

132
00:38:02.273 --> 00:38:28.762
Figure 9: Theoretical A I capability and other task characteristics. Correlations between a research task’s A I capability score percentile and other characteristics of the task. Image description: Bar chart of correlations between a task’s theoretical A I capability percentile and other task characteristics. R L index: +0.78. Cognitive: +0.69. Physical: -0.57.

133
00:38:28.882 --> 00:38:35.216
Interpersonal: -0.31. Regulatory restriction: -0.38.

134
00:38:35.666 --> 00:38:57.033
The same task characteristics also predict how much researchers use Claude for a given task (Figure 10). Claude is barely used for tasks in the bottom fifth of R L suitability. For tasks in the top fifth, Claude use is disproportionately high relative to the time researchers spend on them: twice as high on Claude, and four times as high on Claude Code.

135
00:38:57.153 --> 00:39:07.966
This is similar to the association between Claude use and A I capability scores overall, where the top 20 percent of tasks also draw two to four times the expected use.

136
00:39:08.416 --> 00:39:31.564
Claude use is also disproportionately high in predominantly cognitive tasks. By contrast, Claude use is low for physical and interpersonal tasks. Claude use is also lower for tasks where regulatory barriers limit A I assistance. Overall, across the spectrum of highly specialized research tasks, usage and capability measures are strongly related.

137
00:39:32.014 --> 00:39:50.971
Figure 10: Claude use and task characteristics. Each panel considers a different task characteristic. The vertical axes represent the use ratio for groups of tasks (“bins”) with different scores on that characteristic. The horizontal axes represent a measure of the characteristic’s intensity.

138
00:39:51.091 --> 00:40:12.284
Orange dots represent the average use ratio for tasks in each bin on Claude; blue dots represent the same for Claude Code. Dots at zero represent bins hidden for privacy because too few accounts are responsible for that use. Image description: Five line charts of the use ratio on Claude and Claude Code by task characteristic.

139
00:40:12.404 --> 00:40:35.444
The use ratio rises with a task’s R L index, reaching about 2.2 on Claude and 4.2 on Claude Code at the highest scores, and with its share of cognitive steps, reaching about 1.4 and 1.5. It falls from about 1.2 to near zero as the share of physical steps, the share of interpersonal steps, or the regulatory restriction score increases.

140
00:40:35.894 --> 00:40:49.062
Different scientific fields rely on different mixes of research activities. Does the correlation between usage and capability scores for narrow research tasks help explain why some fields use Claude more than others?

141
00:40:49.182 --> 00:41:05.854
Our data shows it does: a subfield’s Claude use per researcher tends to rise with the average A I capability score across its tasks (correlations of 0.45 for Claude and 0.57 for Claude Code), as shown in Figure 11.

142
00:41:06.304 --> 00:41:28.514
There is also substantial variation across domains, which suggests that Claude use is affected by factors beyond what A I can do. Health sciences researchers use Claude relatively little compared with the A I capability scores of their tasks, possibly because regulation of medical data and research limits the use of A I or makes it harder to integrate.

143
00:41:28.634 --> 00:41:44.357
Researchers in the formal sciences, social sciences, and the arts and humanities use Claude more than the A I capability scores would predict. Much of the work in these fields is done through text and code, which likely facilitates the adoption of A I tools.

144
00:41:44.477 --> 00:41:53.564
Claude use in the physical sciences and life sciences generally tracks A I capability scores more closely, though individual subfields can deviate.

145
00:41:54.014 --> 00:42:17.480
Figure 11: Claude use and theoretical A I capability across subfields. Both panels show each subfield as a dot, sized by the number of researchers in the subfield. The vertical axis shows usage per researcher on a log scale. Usage per researcher is computed as the subfield’s share of research sessions divided by the subfield’s share of total researchers.

146
00:42:17.600 --> 00:42:34.300
The horizontal axis shows the subfield’s A I capability score, computed by aggregating across all of the subfield’s research tasks weighted by researcher hours. The black line shows the best fit, and each panel reports the correlation weighted by subfield size.

147
00:42:34.420 --> 00:42:42.142
The left panel shows the results for research sessions on Claude; the right panel shows the results for research sessions on Claude Code.

148
00:42:42.262 --> 00:43:05.715
Image description: Two bubble charts, one for Claude and one for Claude Code, plotting each subfield’s use per researcher on a log scale against the theoretical A I capability of its research work, with bubbles sized by the number of researchers. The fitted lines slope upward, with weighted correlations of +0.45 for Claude and +0.57 for Claude Code.

149
00:43:05.835 --> 00:43:12.640
Formal science subfields cluster at high capability and high use, while many health science subfields fall below the line.

150
00:43:13.090 --> 00:43:34.804
Theoretical A I capability also predicts how much researchers allow Claude to work autonomously. Figure 12 shows that autonomous use correlates with the theoretical A I capability of the subfield. In other words, not only do subfields with more theoretical A I capability use A I more, but they also use it in a more autonomous manner.

151
00:43:35.254 --> 00:43:53.997
Figure 12: A I autonomy and theoretical A I capability across subfields. Both panels show each subfield as a dot, sized by the number of researchers in the subfield. The left panel represents research sessions on Claude; the right panel represents research sessions on Claude Code.

152
00:43:54.117 --> 00:44:14.869
The vertical axis shows the mean autonomy score for sessions in the subfield, weighted by session volume. The horizontal axis shows the mean theoretical capability of the subfield’s tasks, also weighted by usage. Tasks that are not covered by any session are excluded from the analysis, since they cannot be scored on autonomy.

153
00:44:14.989 --> 00:44:25.975
For legibility, outlier subfields have been dropped, but they are included in the line of best fit and the correlation calculation. Correlations are weighted by subfield size.

154
00:44:26.095 --> 00:44:50.424
Image description: Two bubble charts, one for Claude and one for Claude Code, plotting each subfield’s mean A I autonomy score (on a scale from 1 to 5) against the theoretical A I capability of its research work, with bubbles sized by the number of researchers. The fitted lines slope upward, with weighted correlations of +0.47 for Claude and +0.31 for Claude Code.

155
00:44:51.224 --> 00:44:53.887
Research acceleration scenarios with A I.

156
00:44:54.337 --> 00:45:14.554
The pace of scientific and technological progress depends on good ideas and the means to pursue them. Historically, both have been limited by human capacity. There are only so many researchers, and each has only so much time to formulate a hypothesis, design the right tests, run the analyses, and synthesize the findings.

157
00:45:15.004 --> 00:45:35.292
A I could ease these constraints in two ways. First, it could take on work that researchers now do, allowing them to pursue more ideas. Second, it could contribute ideas that researchers might not otherwise identify. The second may ultimately matter most, but it is much harder to measure, so our analysis focuses on the first.

158
00:45:36.092 --> 00:45:36.842
Approach.

159
00:45:37.292 --> 00:45:50.275
We consider three scenarios of scientific acceleration through A I. Scenario 1 assumes wide adoption of current A I capabilities, as estimated from our survey and the A I capability scores.

160
00:45:50.395 --> 00:46:12.811
Scenarios 2 and 3 are hypothetical futures in which A I fully automates 75 percent or 100 percent of non-physical research steps, the individual actions that make up each research task in SciNet, starting with the steps that have the highest A I capability scores. In all three scenarios, researchers continue to perform physical steps.

161
00:46:12.931 --> 00:46:26.585
These are illustrative scenarios, not forecasts. They show how physical work, theoretical A I capability, and the cost of A I could shape the acceleration of scientific research, and which fields could accelerate the most.

162
00:46:27.035 --> 00:46:45.632
For each scenario, we report two measures. The first is the A I share: the fraction of researcher time spent on a study that A I could take on. Given how researchers currently allocate their time, it measures how much human research time A I could free up, and it follows directly from our analyses above.

163
00:46:45.752 --> 00:47:05.712
Researchers can redirect that freed-up time to other work, increasing research output. Our second measure, the acceleration, estimates this gain: the increase in research output for a fixed research budget, relative to the research output without A I. It tells us how many more research results the same budget could produce.

164
00:47:05.832 --> 00:47:17.032
Freed-up time does not translate one-for-one into more output, both because researchers redirect their effort toward the tasks where A I helps less, and because A I itself has costs.

165
00:47:17.482 --> 00:47:42.186
We build the A I share for Scenario 1 from our survey, which records five levels of A I use: none, assistive, shared, delegated, and autonomous. We assume these levels correspond to A I taking on 0 percent, 25 percent, 50 percent, 75 percent, and 100 percent of a researcher’s time for a given task step. Within each task category, we rank task steps by A I capability score.

166
00:47:42.306 --> 00:48:01.975
We then assign a level of A I use to each task step, in the same proportions reported in our survey, with the highest levels going to the highest-scoring steps. For ideation and hypothesis generation, for example, 17 percent of responses report no A I use, and 53 percent report assistive use.

167
00:48:02.095 --> 00:48:22.034
The 17 percent of ideation task steps with the lowest A I capability scores are therefore assigned no A I share, the next 53 percent are assigned 25 percent, and so on up the scale. Because respondents are likely to be heavy A I adopters, we treat their reported use as a proxy for what A I can do today.

168
00:48:22.154 --> 00:48:28.014
Finally, we aggregate researcher time freed up by A I from steps to fields (see the Appendix ).

169
00:48:28.464 --> 00:48:51.077
To compute the acceleration, we use an ideas production function, a standard economic model of how research inputs, such as researcher time, are turned into research outputs. In this model, a paper requires many research tasks (Jones 2026). The tasks are complementary, such that strength in one cannot fully make up for weakness in another.

170
00:48:51.197 --> 00:49:09.373
As A I makes some tasks cheaper, researchers shift their effort toward tasks where A I helps less, thereby easing the bottlenecks that limit the overall gain. For example, researchers could spend more time coming up with new questions or running more physical experiments to expand empirical evidence.

171
00:49:09.493 --> 00:49:34.359
We calibrate how much these bottlenecks limit the overall gain using Ahmadpoor and Jones (2019). For A I’s cost, we assume that an hour of A I agent time costs 30, dollarscompared with 95 dollarsfor an hour of a researcher’s time, and that A I runs for 0.2 hours for each researcher hour it saves. These are illustrative assumptions: the A I cost uses today’s public list prices.

172
00:49:34.479 --> 00:49:45.544
The smaller these costs, the more research can be done within a given budget and the larger the acceleration, as we discuss below. The Appendix describes the model and assumptions.

173
00:49:45.994 --> 00:49:58.977
Figure 13: Research time freed up by A I and research acceleration. Every row is a field. The left panel shows fields ranked by the estimated share of researcher time that A I could take on.

174
00:49:59.097 --> 00:50:20.771
Circles show estimates for current capabilities; diamonds show the estimated share of time freed up if 75 percent of non-physical steps are automated; triangles show the estimated share of time freed up if 100 percent of non-physical steps are automated. The right panel shows the research acceleration given the research budget of a field.

175
00:50:20.891 --> 00:50:42.540
Its horizontal axis shows research output with A I as a multiple of the output without A I (1 x = no change) under our three scenarios. Image description: Two-panel dot plot of 34 fields under three scenarios: current A I, 75 percent of non-physical steps automated, and all non-physical steps automated.

176
00:50:42.660 --> 00:51:05.679
Left: percentage of research time freed through A I, which ranges from about 27 percent to 52 percent under current A I, with statistics and computer science highest and the arts and anthropology lowest. Right: research output relative to research without A I, on a log scale. Under current A I, acceleration ranges from about 1.35 x to 2 x.

177
00:51:05.799 --> 00:51:21.457
With all non-physical steps automated, statistics, computer science, and most social sciences reach about 15 x, while bench sciences such as biology, chemistry, biomedical sciences, and materials science stay near 3 to 4 x.

178
00:51:22.257 --> 00:51:24.548
Scenario 1: Current capabilities.

179
00:51:24.998 --> 00:51:39.074
With current capabilities, if adopted widely, A I could take on 27 to 52 percent of the researcher time needed to produce a new study, with a median of 38 percent across fields (Figure 13, left panel).

180
00:51:39.194 --> 00:52:07.116
These shares imply research accelerations ranging from 1.35 x in the arts, 1.36 x in anthropology, and 1.41 x in chemistry to 1.96 x in computer science and 2.02 x in statistics (Figure 13, right panel). Accelerations remain limited because A I work today is mostly assistive or shared rather than autonomous, so researchers still spend substantial time on nearly every step.

181
00:52:07.236 --> 00:52:21.976
Even the most A I-ready fields, whose work is almost entirely cognitive, reach only about a 2 x acceleration. These estimates assume that A I diffuses widely and that researchers apply it to every step where it is useful today.

182
00:52:22.426 --> 00:52:46.456
Two factors explain most of the differences across fields. The first is physical work, which A I cannot take on without physical integrations. In lab and field sciences such as the agricultural sciences, biomedical sciences, chemistry, and materials science, 38 to 41 percent of researcher time goes to physical steps, compared with 4 percent in the median field.

183
00:52:46.576 --> 00:52:57.551
The physical share alone explains about one-third of the variation in the A I share. The second factor is the distribution of A I capability scores within cognitive work.

184
00:52:57.671 --> 00:53:13.558
The arts and history spend little time on physical steps (10 percent and 4 percent, respectively), yet their cognitive steps have low A I capability scores, perhaps because qualitative and interpretive work is less amenable to reinforcement learning.

185
00:53:13.678 --> 00:53:25.390
Computer science and statistics, by contrast, have high scores and large A I shares, driven by data analysis, the type of work with the highest A I capability scores across fields.

186
00:53:25.840 --> 00:53:41.438
Fields with a higher use ratio on Claude also tend to have larger A I shares (Figure 14). The A I share is calculated without any Claude use data, so this relationship is further evidence that A I use tracks theoretical A I capability.

187
00:53:41.888 --> 00:54:00.511
Figure 14: Claude use and research time freed up by A I across fields. The correlation between observed Claude use and the A I share for each field. Each field is a dot, sized by the number of researchers. The vertical axis shows the field’s use ratio on Claude, on a log scale.

188
00:54:00.631 --> 00:54:20.684
The horizontal axis shows the estimated share of human research time that could be completed by A I with current capabilities. Image description: Bubble chart of each field’s use ratio on Claude, on a log scale, against the percentage of research time freed through A I, with bubbles sized by the number of researchers.

189
00:54:20.804 --> 00:54:35.802
The fitted line slopes upward, with a weighted correlation of +0.58. Statistics and computer science are highest on both measures. Medicine and clinical sciences and veterinary medicine have the lowest use ratios.

190
00:54:36.602 --> 00:54:39.222
Scenarios 2 and 3: Greater automation.

191
00:54:39.672 --> 00:54:53.535
In these more speculative scenarios, which depend heavily on assumptions about A I’s reach and costs, greater automation raises both the A I share and the acceleration sharply, and widens the differences between fields.

192
00:54:53.985 --> 00:55:17.791
In Scenario 2, where 75 percent of non-physical steps can be done by A I, accelerations reach about 8 x in computer science, 7 x in statistics and library and information science, and 6 x in economics. Most other social sciences, mathematics, and physics and astronomy reach 4 to 5 x, while bench and medical sciences reach about 2 to 3 x.

193
00:55:17.911 --> 00:55:36.891
The bench sciences and the arts and humanities gain the least, but for different reasons. In the bench sciences, much of the work is physical and must still be done by researchers. In the arts and humanities, the work is mostly cognitive but has low A I capability scores, so few of its steps are among those automated.

194
00:55:37.011 --> 00:55:50.440
Automated steps make up 82 percent of non-physical researcher time in computer science but only 28 percent in the arts. History, anthropology, and the arts accelerate by about 2 x or less.

195
00:55:50.890 --> 00:56:09.492
In Scenario 3, the arts and humanities pull ahead, while the bench sciences remain held back. Once A I automates all non-physical steps, low A I capability scores no longer limit cognitive work, and A I can take on nearly all research work in many fields (Figure 13, left panel).

196
00:56:09.612 --> 00:56:24.451
In this speculative scenario, fields with few physical tasks, including statistics, computer science, economics, mathematics, psychology, and most of the social sciences and the arts and humanities, reach about 15 to 16 x.

197
00:56:24.571 --> 00:56:43.535
Fields with substantial lab or field work, such as biology, chemistry, materials science, agricultural sciences, and biomedical sciences, reach only 3 to 4 x. Absent automated labs, their physical steps, representing about 40 percent of researcher time, remain the bottleneck.

198
00:56:43.985 --> 00:57:05.737
Two lessons follow from these scenarios. First, physical bottlenecks remain powerful. Steps that A I cannot perform without physical integrations come to dominate researcher time and budgets, capping acceleration. Second, where no such bottlenecks exist, extraordinary rates of progress become possible, and A I’s cost becomes the limit.

199
00:57:05.857 --> 00:57:26.295
Under our assumptions, a task that is fully automated by A I still costs about one-sixteenth of what it would cost a researcher to perform, so accelerations approach about 16 x and stop there. Lower A I costs would raise this ceiling proportionately, such that halving the cost of A I, for example, would double the rate of progress.

200
00:57:26.415 --> 00:57:35.844
If costs fall sharply, including for harder tasks that A I automates later, the fields without physical bottlenecks could accelerate far beyond these estimates.

201
00:57:36.294 --> 00:57:51.061
These calculations have important limits that cut in both directions. We do not consider constraints at the level of a field rather than a research team. For instance, only so many experiments can run at facilities like the Large Hadron Collider.

202
00:57:51.181 --> 00:58:12.152
We also count only the cost of researcher and A I time, not the costs of machinery or inputs for experiments, which can be substantial in the health, physical, and life sciences. Nor do we consider simulations to replace physical experiments, which could ease bottlenecks in some fields. Finally, we do not consider the quality of ideas.

203
00:58:12.272 --> 00:58:23.474
If A I identifies better hypotheses with higher chances of success, research could accelerate further; if research taste remains uniquely human, the acceleration would be slower.

204
00:58:24.274 --> 00:58:25.799
Researcher expectations.

205
00:58:26.249 --> 00:58:48.811
The scenarios above characterize a range of possibilities. Researchers’ own expectations can provide a unique guide grounded in expertise. To gather their viewpoints, our survey of researchers in the life and health sciences asked when they thought A I, working on its own, would match the best researchers in their field for different categories of research work (Figure 15).

206
00:58:48.931 --> 00:59:13.319
Respondents’ expectations are high in the near term: over half expect A I to match top researchers at data analysis by 2027, and 38 percent say that A I has already reached this level. By 2030, 94 percent expect A I will match the best researchers in data analysis, 89 percent in writing, and 72 percent each in ideation and data gathering.

207
00:59:13.439 --> 00:59:20.943
Expectations for ideation and data gathering are lower, consistent with the lower use of Claude for these tasks today.

208
00:59:21.393 --> 00:59:43.135
Expectations for end-to-end A I research without humans in the loop are more conservative, but researchers still expect remarkable progress. About 40 percent of respondents expect A I to carry out full research projects by 2030, and about 64 percent by 2035, although 16 percent say it will never happen.

209
00:59:43.255 --> 01:00:04.181
Expectations that A I will autonomously produce scientific breakthroughs are more cautious still, with approximately half of researchers expecting this to occur only after 2035, if ever. Overall, many researchers expect high levels of automation in the next decade, broadly in line with our greater automation scenarios.

210
01:00:04.301 --> 01:00:17.421
How fast and to what extent these expectations materialize will depend not only on advances in A I as a software technology, but also on efforts to build automated labs that overcome physical barriers.

211
01:00:17.871 --> 01:00:47.698
Comparing past survey evidence to realized outcomes suggests that A I progress has often been dramatically underestimated (Rosenberg and others 2026). In a survey in mid-2022, for example, the median estimate for A I achieving gold-level performance in the International Mathematical Olympiad was 2030 among domain experts and 2035 among superforecasters (Karger and others 2023).

212
01:00:47.818 --> 01:01:08.849
A I achieved this milestone in 2025, some five to 10 years ahead of those predictions. While forecasters have systematically underestimated progress in A I capabilities, there are a few exceptions, primarily in domains that require execution in the physical world, such as A I-assisted laboratory tasks in biology.

213
01:01:09.299 --> 01:01:26.576
Figure 15: When researchers expect A I to match the best researchers in their field. Results come from our own survey. The top four rows correspond to when researchers expect A I, working on its own, to match the best researchers in their field in different task categories.

214
01:01:26.696 --> 01:01:41.552
The bottom two rows correspond to when researchers expect A I, without a human scientist in the loop, to go “from question to finding” and to produce “a finding your field would regard as a major advance” in their respective fields.

215
01:01:41.672 --> 01:02:04.134
Image description: Stacked bar chart of when survey respondents expect A I to match the best researchers in their field, from already possible today to never. Already possible today: 13 percent for ideation and hypothesis generation, 23 percent for data gathering, 38 percent for data analysis, and 30 percent for writing and communication.

216
01:02:04.254 --> 01:02:20.248
For the full research cycle with no human in the loop, most respondents answer 2030 or later, and 16 percent say never. For a breakthrough finding with no human in the loop, 30 percent say later than 2035 and 18 percent say never.

217
01:02:21.048 --> 01:02:21.836
Discussion.

218
01:02:22.286 --> 01:02:45.582
If A I helps speed up scientific discovery, it could contribute to higher standards of living and faster progress in treating disease. Our estimates suggest the potential gains are substantial. But advancements in A I’s research capabilities do not automatically translate into rapid progress; achieving such acceleration will also depend on how we respond to several constraints.

219
01:02:46.032 --> 01:03:06.766
First, adoption can substantially lag capabilities. Diffusion of new technologies is typically slow (Comin and Hobijn 2010), and productivity gains tend to materialize only after organizations restructure around them (David 1990; Brynjolfsson, Rock, and Syverson 2021).

220
01:03:06.886 --> 01:03:16.363
Individual learning processes, friction at the organizational or system level, and constraints on A I access or infrastructure can all slow adoption.

221
01:03:16.813 --> 01:03:36.482
Second, research may continue to face bottlenecks that A I does not remove. Physical experimentation, which can be costly and slow, can substantially limit progress even with extremely capable A I. We see this especially in the bench sciences in the scenarios above. Choosing what to study may be another bottleneck.

222
01:03:36.602 --> 01:03:52.653
Research questions largely determine a research project’s value, and A I’s capacity to generate good questions remains unclear. If ideation is a bottleneck, it will limit acceleration even as answering a given question becomes much cheaper.

223
01:03:52.773 --> 01:04:04.357
Conversely, if A I outstrips humans in formulating high-quality hypotheses, acceleration could be more substantial, especially in domains where testing hypotheses is costly.

224
01:04:04.807 --> 01:04:22.009
Third, A I-enabled research could raise the quantity or quality of research output, with different implications for science. A surge in new research papers could advance a field faster but also overwhelm scientists’ capacity to absorb or verify the results.

225
01:04:22.129 --> 01:04:45.806
On the other hand, new technologies, from microscopes and telescopes to statistical software, have historically raised the quality of scientific insights, and A I can likewise make more comprehensive analyses and more ambitious projects feasible. And as papers become easier to produce, the research community may increasingly reward work that is especially novel or high quality.

226
01:04:45.926 --> 01:04:56.413
Whether more efficient research leads to greater quantity or greater quality will depend on researchers’ choices and the incentives set by journals, funders, and institutions.

227
01:04:56.863 --> 01:05:14.569
Fourth, the way research is organized may change in ways that are hard to anticipate. As A I becomes more capable and autonomous, A I systems could take the lead in verification, refereeing, and communicating important results, which would ease the absorption problem discussed above.

228
01:05:14.689 --> 01:05:34.776
Further in the future, A I research agents might also push the frontiers of science in large communities of their own, independently of humans, using robotics and automated labs. Such autonomous A I research would raise questions about how humans keep track of new knowledge, whether they can understand it, and how it is generated.

229
01:05:35.226 --> 01:05:56.173
Fifth, more autonomous A I systems may increase the challenge of ensuring that A I-driven research remains aligned with human goals. Keeping humans involved in key research decisions, such as which questions to pursue and how to pursue them, may matter for safety and human wellbeing as A I agents become more powerful and creative.

230
01:05:56.293 --> 01:06:18.256
Discoveries themselves, or the byproducts of research, can also carry risks, particularly in biology, where the same capabilities that speed up medical advances could also produce dangerous knowledge or materials. In such cases, deliberate checkpoints in the research process may be warranted, requiring us to accept slower progress to reduce risk.

231
01:06:18.706 --> 01:06:42.698
Finally, the work that researchers undertake may change substantially. One effect may be a change in researchers’ job satisfaction. If A I takes over enjoyable or rewarding tasks, satisfaction may fall and fewer people may choose research careers. If A I instead takes over tasks that researchers find tedious, satisfaction may rise. Administrative work is one potential target.

232
01:06:42.818 --> 01:07:03.241
In the U S, principal investigators on federally funded projects report spending about 44 percent of their research time meeting administrative requirements rather than doing research (Schneider and others 2020), and easing this burden could be a large boon, saving time and improving overall satisfaction.

233
01:07:03.361 --> 01:07:16.447
Ultimately, the net effect is uncertain and may change as A I’s capabilities evolve. An optimistic view is that A I will expand opportunities for discovery, which is core to what makes research careers rewarding.

234
01:07:16.567 --> 01:07:27.293
If science is a voyage into the “endless frontier,” A I may be a new vessel, allowing researchers to travel farther across a vast expanse of research questions and discoveries.

